      "s65-c6-iadef",
      "What Makes Someone an Investment Adviser",
      "Under the Uniform Securities Act (USA), a \"person\" includes individuals, also called natural persons, and businesses like corporations, partnerships, and sole proprietorships. This chapter covers investment advisers, which are usually companies, and the individuals who work for them.\n\nAn investment adviser (IA) is a business that does all three of these:\n1. Gives advice or analysis about securities\n2. Is in the business of giving that advice, even if it is not its main service\n3. Gets paid for it\nIn short, an IA sells investment advice for a fee. Under the USA, an IA must register in each state where it does business.\n\nThe USA says these are NOT investment advisers:\n• An investment adviser representative, who is an individual working for an IA\n• A bank, savings institution, or trust company\n• A lawyer, accountant, teacher, or engineer (LATE) whose advice is only incidental to their profession\n• A broker-dealer or agent whose advice is only incidental to its brokerage business and who gets no special pay for the advice\n• A publisher of a real newspaper, newsletter, magazine, or financial publication, in print or online, as long as the advice is not tailored to each reader's situation\n• A person who advises only on U.S. government securities and certain other exempt securities\n• A family office that advises only family clients, is owned by them, and does not advertise to the public\n• Anyone else the Administrator excludes by rule or order\n• A federal covered adviser",
