      "When an Adviser Can Skip State Registration",
      "Advisers that are not exempt must register in the states where they do business, so each state Administrator can track and examine them. Skipping a required registration can bring big fines.\n\nAn adviser with no place of business in a state does not have to register there if:\n1. Its only clients there are institutional clients. These include investment companies, investment advisers, broker-dealers, banks, trust companies, savings and loans, insurance companies, and employee benefit plans with at least $1 million in assets. Or:\n2. It has had no more than five retail clients in the state in the past 12 months. This is the de minimis exemption.\n\nAn adviser or its representative also does not need to register in a state just to advise a client who is visiting there temporarily.",
